{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nGitHub code-scanning alert #88 (`py/incomplete-url-substring-sanitization`, Security/quality) flags the upstream-URL assertion in `test_notice_names_pandoc_its_version_and_its_licence` in `tests/unit/test_notice_records_pandoc_gpl.py` (currently line 51):\n\n```python\nassert \"pandoc.org\" in text or \"github.com/jgm/pandoc\" in text, (...)\n```\n\nThis is not real URL sanitization — there is no untrusted input; the test checks that NOTICE mentions upstream so the GPL source-availability obligation is actionable. But the finding is still worth fixing: bare substring containment accepts a NOTICE that only contains a lookalike host — prefix-style (`https://notpandoc.org`) or suffix-style (`https://pandoc.org.evil.com`) — which does NOT actionably point at upstream. The same rule class was fixed before in `test_llm_fallback_chain.py` (ticket revealable-ungracious-billygoat, alerts 81/82) with parsed-origin netloc equality.\n\n## Approach\nReplace the two substring checks with a single scheme+host-anchored regex whose host ends with an explicit end-of-host boundary (a negative lookahead), so BOTH lookalike classes are rejected:\n\n```python\n_UPSTREAM_RE = re.compile(\n    r\"https?://(www\\.)?pandoc\\.org(?![\\w.-])|https?://github\\.com/jgm/pandoc(?![\\w-])\"\n)\nassert _UPSTREAM_RE.search(text), (...)\n```\n\n`(?![\\w.-])` after `pandoc\\.org` refuses to end the host before a dot, letter, digit, or hyphen, so `https://pandoc.org.evil.com` is rejected (unlike a `\\b` anchor, which matches before a literal dot) while `https://pandoc.org`, `https://pandoc.org/`, and `http://www.pandoc.org` all match. `(?![\\w-])` after the github path rejects `https://github.com/jgm/pandoc-extras` (a different repo) while allowing a trailing `/`, whitespace, or end of text. This handles arbitrary subdomain sequences per the CodeQL guidance, matching the strictness of the parsed-origin prior art without urlparse machinery — appropriate because the subject is a whole document, not a single URL to validate. Alternative weighed: extracting every URL from NOTICE and comparing parsed netlocs exactly, as `test_llm_fallback_chain.py` does — rejected as over-engineering for a one-line document-content assertion; the lookahead-anchored regex achieves the same rejection set here.\n\nRED-first demonstration (already executed): the old expression accepts both `https://notpandoc.org.evil.com` (prefix) and `https://pandoc.org.evil.com` (suffix); the anchored regex rejects both and still accepts the real NOTICE.\n\n## Scope\n- `tests/unit/test_notice_records_pandoc_gpl.py` — only the upstream-URL assertion in `test_notice_names_pandoc_its_version_and_its_licence` (plus the module-level compiled regex it uses). Do NOT touch the pandoc version pin strings or NOTICE content (a parallel agent is bumping pypandoc-binary in the same file; anchoring the change to the assertion, not a line number, keeps the two changes rebase-clean).\n\nSibling sweep (already executed): alert #88 is the only open code-scanning alert repo-wide; grep for URL/hostname substring-`in` checks found no other same-class occurrences in scope (remaining matches are scheme-anchored full-URL literals or error-message assertions).\n\n## Testing\nTest-only change. Happy path: `pytest tests/unit/test_notice_records_pandoc_gpl.py -x -q` stays green against the real NOTICE. Failure path: the RED demonstration shows the anchored regex rejects `https://pandoc.org.evil.com`, `https://notpandoc.org`, and a bare schemeless `pandoc.org` mention, all of which (except the last two prefix forms partially) satisfied the old expression.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] The upstream-URL assertion in `test_notice_names_pandoc_its_version_and_its_licence` no longer uses bare substring containment; it matches via a scheme+host-anchored regex with an explicit end-of-host boundary. Verify: read the diff.\n- [x] The new assertion rejects a suffix-style lookalike: `_UPSTREAM_RE.search(\"https://pandoc.org.evil.com\")` is None. Verify: run the expression in Python.\n- [x] The new assertion rejects a prefix-style lookalike: `_UPSTREAM_RE.search(\"https://notpandoc.org\")` is None. Verify: run the expression in Python.\n- [x] The new assertion accepts the real NOTICE, preserving the test's intent that NOTICE actionably points at upstream. Verify: `pytest tests/unit/test_notice_records_pandoc_gpl.py -x -q` passes.\n- [x] The diff touches only the upstream-URL assertion and its supporting regex; version pins and NOTICE content are unchanged. Verify: read the diff.\n- [x] `make lint` passes. Verify: run it.\n- [x] `make typecheck` passes. Verify: run it.\n- [x] The change is pushed to Gerrit for review. Verify: a Gerrit change exists for the commit.\n- [ ] After the fix lands and CodeQL re-runs, alert #88 clears without dismissal. Verify: `gh api repos/navapbc/rebar/code-scanning/alerts` shows no open alert #88."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786775016506974001,"uuid":"d9c3d705-53bf-44e5-895c-00e230717cd7"}